<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masyaf</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masyaf is a town in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on the eastern slopes of the coastal mountains west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, dominated by a medieval castle. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismaili-state">Nizari Ismailis</a> acquired the castle in 1140–1141, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashid-al-din-sinan">Rashid al-Din Sinan</a> it was the headquarters of their Syrian branch, the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>' of crusader sources; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> besieged it without success in 1176. It passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baybars">Baybars</a> around 1270, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismailism">Ismaili</a> community still lives in the area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masyaf.jpg" alt="Masyaf" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amjad helo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masyaf_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
